ontent.com/u/180032?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou","html_url":"https://github.com/Calinou","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Calinou/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-02-06T00:31:22Z","updated_at":"2026-02-06T00:32:29Z","body":"> D3D12 unfortunately does not support nearest anisotropic filtering\r\n\r\nFor future reference, this is also not *consistently* supported on Vulkan (possibly even OpenGL). Only NVIDIA really supports it well on those APIs, while it will generally not work as expected on Intel/AMD GPUs. See https://github.com/UZDoom/UZDoom/issues/194.\r\n\r\nFaking nearest-neighbor filtering in a shader is likely the way to go if you want anisotropic filtering on pixel art. This also gives you the opportunity to implement antialiasing on the internal texels (something MSAA won't do). See also https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/88650.","author_association":"MEMBER","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/3857132254/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/4006420879","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/115504#issuecomment-4006420879","issue_url":"https://api